course: dictionary
lesson: 9914
title: Working out a plant word from clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a new word from the words around it. Look for clues. «The camel ate sticks and thorns and tamarisks and milkweed and prickles.» Sticks, thorns, milkweed and prickles are all plants or prickly things, so tamarisks must be a kind of plant too.
explain: A tamarisk is a shrub or small tree with feathery leaves and small pink or white flowers. It grows well in dry, salty places such as seasides and deserts. Those facts match the clues in the sentence, so you can trust your guess.
explain: Always check your guess. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the hard word: «The camel ate sticks and thorns and plants and milkweed and prickles.» It still makes sense, so your guess is right. If it does not fit, or the clues are not enough, look the word up in a dictionary.
fact: A tamarisk is a shrub or small tree with feathery leaves and small pink or white flowers.
fact: Tamarisks grow well in dry, salty places such as seasides and deserts, and by salt lakes.
fact: Tamarisk roots are long, and they can find water deep underground.
fact: In the Bible, Abraham planted a tamarisk tree at Beersheba.
fact: In parts of the USA and Australia, tamarisk is a weed that spreads and drinks river water.
exercise: «The camel ate sticks and thorns and tamarisks and milkweed and prickles.» What is a tamarisk? | A type of rain cloud | *A shrub or small tree with feathery leaves | A kind of sand dune | A large animal that lives in deserts
exercise: Where do tamarisks grow well? | *In dry, salty places such as deserts and seasides | On the tops of snowy hills | Inside dark caves in the mountains | Deep under the sea, in cold water
exercise: In the story, «the camel ate sticks and thorns and tamarisks and milkweed and prickles». What did the camel eat? | Fish from the sea | Ice cream and cake | Fresh bread from the baker | *Sticks, thorns and tamarisks
test: In parts of the USA and Australia, tamarisk is seen as what? | A rare flower kept in a glasshouse | *A weed that spreads and drinks river water | A seaside fish | A type of desert sand
test: Why are tamarisk roots useful in a desert? | *They can reach water deep underground | They shade the sand from the sun | They are sharp enough to cut rock | They float on salt lakes
exam: Which sentence best explains what a tamarisk is? | A type of rain cloud | A kind of sand dune | A large animal that lives in deserts | *A shrub or small tree with feathery leaves and small pink or white flowers
